\begin_layout Standard
a.) Determine the object's total distance traveled and displacement.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
b.) At t = 0, the object's position is x = 2 m.
 Find the object's position at t = 2, t = 4, t = 7, and t = 10.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
c.) What is the object's acceleration at the following times: t = 1, t =
 3, and t = 6.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
d.) Sketch the corresponding acceleration vs.
 time graph from t = 0 to t = 10.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Flex Strong
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Solution
\end_layout

\end_inset

 : a.) Recall that the equation for velocity is v = x/t.
 If we solve this for x, we get x = vt.
 Notice that this is the same as the area of a rectangles whose sides are
 lengths v and t, so we can determine that the displacement is the area
 enclosed by the velocity vs.
 time graph.
 So, we will find the area of each section under the graph:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Box Doublebox
position "t"
hor_pos "c"
has_inner_box 1
inner_pos "t"
use_parbox 0
use_makebox 0
width "72col%"
special "none"
