status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\begin_inset Graphics
	filename I:/DataBack/projects/Images/CC/P-Graphs/4.1e/Images/Kinematics/ex23a.jpg
	scale 50

\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The total distance traveled by the object is simply the sum of all these
 areas: 3 + 6 + 4.5 + 2 + 2 = 17.5 m The displacement is found in a similar
 fashion, except areas below the x-axis are considered negative: 3 + 6 +
 4.5 - 2 - 2 = 9.5 m Interestingly enough, the area enclosed by any function
 can be represented by a definite integral.
 For example, if this graph were defined as a function v(t), then the displaceme
nt would be the integral from 0 to 10 of v(t)dt, and the total distance
 traveled would be the integral from 0 to 10 of |v(t)|dt Go to calculus
 notes
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
b.) The position of the object at a given point in time can be found in much
 the same way we found the displacement in part a, except this time we must
 also add in the initial value given.
 So: x(2) = 2 + 3 = 5 m x(4) = 2 + 3 + 6 = 11 m x(7) = 2 + 3 + 6 + 4.5 =
 15.5 m x(10) = 2 + 3 + 6 + 4.5 - 2 - 2 = 11.5 m
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Notice that this can also be done by adding the integral from 0 to t of
 v(t)dt to the initial value of 2.
 Go to calculus notes
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
c.) Like velocity in part b of problem 22, the instantaneous acceleration
 at any point along one of the graph's line segments is the same as the
 average acceleration across that line segment.
 The formula for acceleration is aavg = Δv/Δt = (vf - vi)/(tf - ti), so:
 a(t) = (vf - vi)/(tf - ti) a(1) = (3 - 0)/(2 - 0) = 3/2 = 1.5 m/s2 a(3)
 = (3 - 3)/(4 - 2) = 0/2 = 0 m/s2 a(6) = (-2 - 3)/(9 - 4) = -5/5 = -1 m/s2
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Similarly to the relationship between velocity and position, the formula
 for acceleration is the same as the slope formula for a velocity vs.
 time graph.
 So, we can say that the slope of any velocity vs.
 time graph is its acceleration.
 Notice that this definition defines acceleration as the derivative of velocity.
 So, it is true that for any velocity function v(t), its derivative is an
 acceleration function a(t).
 Also, integration can be used to go from an acceleration function to a
 position function.
 Go to calculus notes
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
d.) We know that the acceleration along each line segment of this velocity
 vs.
 time graph is equal to the slope of the line segment.
 We determined these slopes in part c, so the acceleration graph would look
